  "commit": "52fbc261f3011f2d98303ceefa25b77352a23d48",
  "description": "Three v1 items closed and a fourth left in flight, but the run's real subject is how much of what was argued turned out to be wrong. It opened by acting on the criticism that UI items were merging without ever running in the container: the topbar shell, merged earlier on a Blade assertion alone, was finally driven in the image, and a rule went into CLAUDE.md so the remaining fourteen UI items would follow. The rule was then mutation-tested and half of it did not survive -- see decision/0012 -- which is the pattern the whole run repeats. Email folding was not the comparison inconsistency the issue described but an installer lockout: the one path that creates the first administrator stored the address as typed while login always folded it, so on SQLite and PostgreSQL that account could not log in at all, and the tests proving it were shown failing on a throwaway branch before the fix landed. Two of those tests then failed on MySQL for the opposite reason, having asserted folding through a where() clause whose meaning changes by driver -- decision/0010 recurring inside the tests written for its own item, the third time that pattern has appeared. FilePolicy was raising a TypeError rather than returning a boolean for every file in a trashed subtree, and was split by intent rather than blanket-ruled so that a cascade-trashed file is neither revealed through view nor left permanently unrecoverable. Reverse-proxy readiness shipped only after CI refuted two documented arguments: that reading the environment outside config was acceptable here, and that forcing a root URL without also forcing the scheme was redundant. Separately, and in no item, the job named validate ledger was found to have been green since the day it was written without running a single assertion, because it passed the ledger file to a script that takes the ledger directory and the absent-path escape hatch swallowed it silently. The ledger itself was never unvalidated, the test suite covers it on all six legs, but a check that looks like protection and is not is worse than none. It has no item because the ledger has no item for its own tooling, which is a gap worth closing. The extraction flake that cost an unrelated PR a full cycle closed last, and it too was recorded Active while in flight -- the first item ever to be, so that work in progress is visible in the ledger rather than indistinguishable from work never started. Its stated cause did not survive review: the smoke uploads through the web UI before extraction runs, and that upload writes to the object store synchronously, so storage was demonstrably serving before the read failed. The cause remains unknown. Retrying three times inside one second is indefensible whatever it turns out to be, and the smoke now prints the error the job had been recording all along, which is why this one was reasoned about from supervisor timings instead of read off the exception. Its test cost three rounds of CI, one of them spent on a fix of mine that was a guess and did not hold, before being restructured to call the method under test directly: a test routed through a queue fake, the bus, container injection and a bound double can only ever report that no exception was thrown, and never which of the four ate it.",
